{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2410 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a7990fd294abd424662be75239e8e5ef70391daf5b40067480a6ed11ab5b596c" :edb/db-before-t 2409 :edb/db-after-t 2410 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535722 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:55:23.572Z" 13194139535722 true] [17592186149818 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149820 13194139535722 true] [17592186149821 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149822 13194139535722 true] [17592186149824 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149825 13194139535722 true] [17592186149827 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149828 13194139535722 true] [17592186149831 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149832 13194139535722 true] [17592186149834 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149836 13194139535722 true] [17592186149837 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149838 13194139535722 true] [17592186149841 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149843 13194139535722 true] [17592186152207 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363198 13194139535722 true] [17592186152208 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363205 13194139535722 true] [17592186152491 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535722 true] [17592186152491 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+4y(x)=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" 13194139535722 true] [17592186152491 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y^{″}(x) + 4y(x) = 0, y(0) = y(1) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le 1$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" 13194139535722 false] [17592186152491 1145 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=0$ and $x=b=1.$\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nThese are Dirichlet boundary conditions." 13194139535722 true] [17592186152492 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535722 true] [17592186152492 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139535722 true] [17592186152492 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″}(x) + x^{2}y^{′}(x) + y(x) = 2x, 0 \\le x \\le π$\n\n$2y(0) + y^{′}(0) = 0, y(π) + 2y^{′}(π) = 0$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\nThe governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\nFor this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\nThe boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139535722 false] [17592186152492 1145 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)=2x≢0,$ the equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Robin}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is inhomogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139535722 true] [17592186152493 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535722 true] [17592186152493 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139535722 true] [17592186152493 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″}(x) + \\sin (x)y^{′}(x) + 3y(x) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le 2$\n\n$y^{′}(0) = 2, y^{′}(2) =-1$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\nThe governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\nFor this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\nThe boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139535722 false] [17592186152493 1145 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Neumann}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139535722 true] [17592186363198 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363199 13194139535722 true] [17592186363198 1089 "q-338618" 13194139535722 true] [17592186363198 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535722 true] [17592186363198 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+4y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,2y(-2)-{y}^{′}(-2)=3,\\,y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=-1,\\,-2\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nWhich of the 